- deferred-close-hangs-on-established-client-http (lws-protocol.c:~50,
  pollfd_handler's forced-service while loop; ops-h1.c:719-747,
  rops_handle_POLLIN_h1): closing an HTTP client wsi from
  onEstablishedClientHttp via the same deferred-close trick used to fix
  raw-client-immediate-close-fires-error-not-close/test-websocket-hs-
  rejected-on-sync-close (js_invoke_deferred(), lws-socket.c) doesn't
  just misroute the event here, it live-locks the whole process at
  100% CPU forever - confirmed via verbose logging showing
  "RECEIVE_CLIENT_HTTP (swallowed N dupes)" climbing into the hundreds
  of thousands per second, and that not even an unrelated JS-level
  setTimeout() ever fires again. Root cause: once HTTP headers finish
  parsing, ops-h1.c's rops_handle_POLLIN_h1 sets wsi->client_rx_avail=1,
  explicitly disables POLLIN for the wsi, and fires
  LWS_CALLBACK_RECEIVE_CLIENT_HTTP - the contract (per its own comment,
  "he'll usually ask for writeable callback and drain / re-enable it
  there") is that the handler must call lws_http_client_read()
  (wsi.httpClientRead()) to drain the response body, which is also what
  re-arms polling. A handler that never does that (as
  onEstablishedClientHttp-only code naturally wouldn't) leaves
  client_rx_avail set forever; pollfd_handler's forced-service loop
  (while(lws_service_adjust_timeout(...) == 0) lws_service_tsi(...);,
  the external-poll-integration pattern libwebsockets itself documents)
  assumes forced service always makes bounded progress and never
  returns to the JS/event-loop layer until it doesn't need to run again
  - here it never stops needing to run, so it spins in native C forever.
  Deferring the close (making onEstablishedClientHttp report success
  instead of triggering the old, synchronous-reject-and-close path via
  bail3_l in client-http.c) is what exposes this: the old
  misrouted-but-real close tore the wsi down immediately via
  lws_close_free_wsi(), before ever reaching the
  wait-for-body-consumption state this hangs in. Not fixed: would need
  either draining/discarding the pending body before or instead of
  deferring the close, or capping the forced-service loop's iteration
  count - both bigger, riskier changes than this session's scope.
  Deliberately left out of the RAW_CONNECTED/CLIENT_ESTABLISHED switch in
  lwsjs_socket_close().

    import { LWSContext, createServer, LWS_WRITE_HTTP_FINAL, LWSMPRO_CALLBACK } from 'lws.so';
    const server = createServer({
      port: 29990, vhostName: 'localhost',
      mounts: [{ mountpoint: '/', protocol: 'http', originProtocol: LWSMPRO_CALLBACK }],
      protocols: [{ name: 'http', onHttp(wsi) { wsi.respond(200, 4, {}); wsi.write('body', LWS_WRITE_HTTP_FINAL); } }],
    });
    const ctx = new LWSContext({
      protocols: [{ name: 'http', onEstablishedClientHttp(wsi) { wsi.close(); } }], // would hang if wired into the switch - no onReceiveClientHttp to drain the body
    });
    ctx.clientConnect({ address: 'localhost', port: 29990, path: '/', host: 'localhost', method: 'GET', protocol: 'http' });

- async-dns-qtype-ignored-on-cache-fill (libwebsockets/lib/system/async-dns/async-dns.c,
  used via lws_async_dns_query() from the new ctx.resolve() binding in
  lws-context.c): querying the same hostname with qtype AAAA after (or
  instead of) qtype A returns both the A and AAAA records mixed together
  in a single result, rather than only the records matching the requested
  type. Confirmed against a real external name (example.com, via the
  sandbox's systemd-resolved stub at 127.0.0.53) - both { type: 'A' } and
  { type: 'AAAA' } queries in separate fresh LWSContexts came back with
  the same four addresses (two IPv4, two IPv6). Not narrowed further -
  could be the DNS cache keying by name only and not by qtype, or the
  query construction itself not restricting the wire request to one
  record type. Not in scope to fix here since it's inside the vendored
  libwebsockets submodule, not this project's own binding code.

    const ctx = new LWSContext({});
    console.log(await ctx.resolve('example.com', { type: 'AAAA' }));
    // -> includes IPv4 addresses too, not just AAAA/IPv6

- false-positive-unhandled-rejection-warning (qjsm interpreter, promise job
  queue): `await`ing a synchronously-throwing async function still prints
  "Possibly unhandled promise rejection: <Error>" to stderr even when the
  `await` is directly inside a `try { ... } catch { ... }` that correctly
  catches it - cosmetic (the catch block does run, execution continues
  normally), but noisy. Found while testing the new SEARCH: request
  channel's error path (lib/web-search.js's webSearch() throwing
  synchronously when GOOGLE_API_KEY/GOOGLE_CSE_ID aren't set, caught by
  lib/tool-requests.js's runRequests()). Reproduces with a two-line async
  function unrelated to this project's own code, both at top level and
  nested inside another async function - an interpreter/job-queue
  diagnostic quirk, not specific to ollama-repl. Not investigated further
  or fixed - would need digging into quickjs's own promise rejection
  tracking, out of scope here.

    async function f() { throw new Error('boom'); }
    try { await f(); } catch(e) { console.log('caught:', e.message); }
    // prints "Possibly unhandled promise rejection: Error: boom" to
    // stderr anyway, then "caught: boom" - the catch worked fine, the
    // warning is just wrong

- serve-upgrade-hangs-in-fuller-scenario (lib/serve.js, server.upgrade() / makeUpgradeHook()): 
  server.upgrade() works in every focused test tried - a raw createServer()+WsProtocol+upgrade-hook repro, a raw createServer()+WebSocket.protocol()+upgrade-hook repro, and a minimal serve({fetch, websocket}) test with a single /ws endpoint and ws.data all connected and exchanged messages correctly - but a fuller end-to-end test (multiple endpoints, a preceding plain fetch() request, an assert-heavy client harness) left the WS client hanging with no open/error/close event ever firing, no native LLL_USER log line for the attempt at all, and no clear repro reduced yet before this was set aside to finish the rest of the task. Something about the fuller scenario's exact shape triggers it; isolated pieces of that same scenario (plain fetch before connecting, URL parsing in the fetch handler, the connect()-via-Promise client helper) were each individually ruled out as the sole cause without finding what combination actually does it. Needs a fresh, patient bisection of the original failing test before server.upgrade() can be trusted beyond the single-endpoint case.

    not minimally reduced yet - see the description above for what was and wasn't ruled out; start from a serve({fetch, websocket}) with two distinct upgrade-accepting URL branches and a preceding plain fetch() call, then bisect down

- h1-late-queued-pipeline-never-promoted (libwebsockets/lib/roles/h1/ops-h1.c,
  rops_handle_POLLOUT_h1; contrast libwebsockets/lib/core-net/wsi.c:1720,
  lws_wsi_mux_apply_queue): native root cause of the ollama-repl "only the
  first prompt works" symptom (mitigated at the application layer - see
  IDLE_RECONNECT_MS in examples/ollama-repl/lib/ollama-client.js/
  gemini-client.js, and tests/test-keepalive.js's investigation - but not
  fixable there, this lives entirely in vendored libwebsockets). When a
  second HTTP/1.1 client request is queued onto an existing LCCSCF_PIPELINE
  connection *after* the first transaction has already fully completed and
  gone idle (as opposed to being queued while the first is still in
  flight), it never gets sent: lws_client_connect_4_established()
  (core-net/client/connect4.c:147) parks it on the parent wsi's
  dll2_cli_txn_queue_owner in state LRS_H2_WAITING_TO_SEND_HEADERS and
  calls lws_callback_on_writable(parent) - but nothing ever promotes it out
  of that state for a plain H1 parent. The promotion logic that exists,
  lws_wsi_mux_apply_queue() (core-net/wsi.c:1720), only checks
  lwsi_role_h2()/h3//mqtt roles (called from roles/h2/http2.c,
  roles/mqtt/mqtt.c, the quic files) - plain H1's own POLLOUT handler,
  rops_handle_POLLOUT_h1 (roles/h1/ops-h1.c), never touches
  dll2_cli_txn_queue_owner at all. The "back-to-back, still in flight"
  case works fine because that handoff happens synchronously inside
  lws_http_transaction_completed_client() (roles/http/client/
  client-http.c:515) as transaction #1 itself completes - a wholly
  different path that only fires if the queue is non-empty at that exact
  moment. Confirmed via LLL_USER logging on a live ollama-repl session:
  the second POST shows ESTABLISHED_CLIENT_HTTP (fired early/spuriously at
  queue time) and SERVER_NEW_CLIENT_INSTANTIATED, but never
  CLIENT_APPEND_HANDSHAKE_HEADER (where HttpClientProtocol sets
  wsi.bodyPending=1 and writes the real request) - the queued child just
  sits until a pending-timeout tears it down as CLIENT_HTTP_DROP_PROTOCOL.
  Not fixed here: would need an H1-specific equivalent of
  lws_wsi_mux_apply_queue(), called from rops_handle_POLLOUT_h1 (or
  wherever the parent's writable event is actually dispatched for h1),
  in vendored libwebsockets - a bigger, riskier native change than this
  pass attempted.

    tests/test-keepalive.js's fixture server can't
    reproduce this directly (its keepalive_timeout closes cleanly, a real
    FIN, which the client notices right away - a different failure mode);
    reproduce against a real Ollama server instead: qjsm
    examples/ollama-repl/repl.js --provider ollama --model
    qwen2.5-coder:latest -x -x (second -x for LLL_USER), send one prompt,
    wait ~90s past the reply, send a second - watch for
    ESTABLISHED_CLIENT_HTTP/SERVER_NEW_CLIENT_INSTANTIATED with no
    subsequent CLIENT_APPEND_HANDSHAKE_HEADER before CLIENT_HTTP_DROP_PROTOCOL

- h2-window-update-overflow-fix-option-breaks-connect (libwebsockets/lib/tls/openssl/openssl-client.c:513):
  setting the vhost option LWS_SERVER_OPTION_H2_JUST_FIX_WINDOW_UPDATE_OVERFLOW
  (meant to clamp an oversized incoming h2 WINDOW_UPDATE instead of
  GOAWAY-ing the connection - see roles/h2/http2.c around
  LWS_H2_FRAME_TYPE_WINDOW_UPDATE) makes every client TLS connect fail
  immediately with SSL_ERROR_SYSCALL (errno 9, EBADF) before any h2
  traffic is even exchanged - confirmed with a clean A/B in a minimal
  qjs-lws script (examples/ollama-repl/lib/lws/context.js +
  lib/lws/protocols.js's httpClient): identical connect() call to the
  same https URL succeeds through TLS/ALPN and only then hits the
  WINDOW_UPDATE-overflow warning without the option, but fails at
  SSL_connect() itself as soon as the option bit is added to `options`.
  Root cause not yet isolated (checked: the option's JS binding uses a
  proper 64-bit to_int64free() conversion in lws-context.c, not a
  32-bit-truncating one, and grepping for other users of bit 31 /
  0x80000000 in core-net/tls turned up nothing that would collide).
  Note: the WINDOW_UPDATE overflow this option exists to fix was itself
  seen against two unrelated real hosts (api.mistral.ai and
  example.com) with byte-for-byte identical numbers
  (0x10000 + 0x7fff0000 = 0x80000000), which looks more like this dev
  sandbox's outbound HTTPS proxy reterminating TLS with its own h2 stack
  than genuine server behavior - so this may only be reproducible inside
  a similar intercepting-proxy environment, not against a real origin
  server directly.

    import createContext from './lib/lws/context.js';
    import { httpClient } from './lib/lws/protocols.js';
    import { LCCSCF_PIPELINE, LWS_SERVER_OPTION_CREATE_VHOST_SSL_CTX, LWS_SERVER_OPTION_DO_SSL_GLOBAL_INIT, LWS_SERVER_OPTION_H2_JUST_FIX_WINDOW_UPDATE_OVERFLOW, LWS_SERVER_OPTION_IGNORE_MISSING_CERT } from 'lws.so';
    const adapter = httpClient((req, resp) => console.log('status', resp.status), { error: (req, err) => console.log('error:', err.message) });
    const ctx = createContext({
      options: LWS_SERVER_OPTION_DO_SSL_GLOBAL_INIT | LWS_SERVER_OPTION_CREATE_VHOST_SSL_CTX | LWS_SERVER_OPTION_IGNORE_MISSING_CERT | LWS_SERVER_OPTION_H2_JUST_FIX_WINDOW_UPDATE_OVERFLOW,
      protocols: [{ name: 'http', ...adapter }], timeout_secs: 15,
    });
    await adapter.connect(ctx, 'https://example.com/', { method: 'GET', ssl_connection: LCCSCF_PIPELINE }); // logs "error: connect SYSCALL 9"

